{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789820809,"nonce":"tKCFpeFf5KoMve5bZLfkJn8YVlk=","title":"Python: a list read out of a tuple by a literal index was the box, not the list","message":"`tuple_field_storage` boxes list, dict and set fields (a312aff), and\n`tuple_fields` reads them back through `trusted`, but the literal-index\npath in `subscript` (`pair[0]`) read the slot at the field's type\ndirectly: the box pointer was used as the list header. `pair = ([1], 'x');\nfirst = pair[0]; print(first)` read length and data pointer out of the\nbox's words and crashed (SIGBUS) or printed garbage.\n\nFix: the slot is read as the shape stores it and converted with\n`trusted`, as `tuple_fields` does.","files":null}]}